 of non-hormonal contraception by the patient and/or partner. Contraception use must continue for the duration of study treatment and for at least 6 months after the last dose of study treatment. Male patients whose partners are pregnant should use condoms for the duration of the study. \n\n\n### Response:\nInclusion Criteria:\nHistologically [OR] or cytologically confirmed adenocarcinoma of the breast [AND] with locally advanced [OR] or metastatic disease, [AND] and a candidate for chemotherapy. \nHuman epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)-positive. \n [NOT] No prior chemotherapy [AND] for their metastatic breast cancer (MBC). \nMeasurable disease. \nAge ≥ 18 years. \nFor women [AND] of childbearing potential [AND] and men [AND] with partners of childbearing potential, agreement to use a highly effective, [NOT] non-hormonal form of contraception [OR] or 2 effective forms of [NOT] non-hormonal contraception by the patient and/or partner. Contraception use must continue for the duration of study treatment [AND] and for at least 6 months after the last dose of study treatment. Male patients [AND] whose partners are pregnant should use condoms for the duration of the study.<|eot_id|>']
Response: Inclusion Criteria:
Histologically [OR] or cytologically confirmed adenocarcinoma of the breast [AND] with locally advanced [OR] or metastatic disease, [AND] and a candidate for chemotherapy. 
Human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)-positive. 
 [NOT] No prior chemotherapy [AND] for their metastatic breast cancer (MBC). 
Measurable disease. 
Age ≥ 18 years. 
For women [AND] of childbearing potential [AND] and men [AND] with partners of childbearing potential, agreement to use a highly effective, [NOT] non-hormonal form of contraception [OR] or 2 effective forms of [NOT] non-hormonal contraception by the patient and/or partner. Contraception use must continue for the duration of study treatment [AND] and for at least 6 months after the last dose of study treatment. Male patients [AND] whose partners are pregnant should use condoms for the duration of the study.
