Output: ['<|begin_of_text|>Below is an instruction that describes a task, paired with an input that provides further context. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.\n\n### Instruction:\nCreate an edited version of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria that incorporates the logical operators [AND], [OR], [NOT].\nApply these rules for inserting the logical operators:\n- Always write an [OR] before an "or", "and/or", "and / or" in the text.\n- Always write an [OR] before a comma "," and before "/" in the text.\n- Always write an [AND] before words like "with", "to", "who", "in addition", "for", "and", "but", "that", "despite", "having", "of", "on", "may", "as", "within", "by", "requiring", "should", "must have", "during", "have", "must", "are", "due", "according", "corresponding", "including".\n- Always write an [AND] after "history of".\n- Always write a [NOT] before "no", "not", "do not", "other than", "none",  "don\'t", "free", "besides", "prevent", "Inability", "absence", "impossible", "off", "without", "unable", "naive", "excluded", "absence"\n\nGeneral rules:\n- The operators [OR], [AND], [NOT] should be written before the words like or, and, not.\n- An [AND], [OR], [NOT] must not come before a line segment (\\n).\n- Please carefully read through the given criteria text, then output only an edited version with the logical operators incorporated, enclosed in <EDITED_CRITERIA> tags.\n- Do not include any other explanations or output.\n\n\n### Input:\n1. Taking a tetracycline within 6 months or history of adverse reaction to minocycline or another tetracycline. \n2. Enhanced risk from lumbar puncture, including documented or suspected cerebral mass lesion predisposing to brain herniation or bleeding diathesis. \n3. Pregnancy or expectation of pregnancy during the study. \n4. Active opportunistic infection or active neurological disease that might confound evaluation. \n5. ADC Stage > 1. \n6. Hemoglobin < 10 Gms/dL. \n7. BUN or creatine above the normal limits. \n8. Taking other drugs known to reduce the metabolism of minocycline and thus increase the probability of toxicity.       \n    \n\n\n### Response:\nInclusion Criteria:\n 1. Taking a tetracycline within 6 months [OR] or history of adverse reaction to minocycline [OR] or another tetracycline.\n 2. Enhanced risk from lumbar puncture, including documented [OR] or suspected cerebral mass lesion predisposing to brain herniation [OR] or bleeding diathesis.\n 3. Pregnancy [OR] or expectation of pregnancy during the study.\n 4. Active opportunistic infection [OR] or active neurological disease that might confound evaluation.\n 5. ADC Stage > 1.\n 6. Hemoglobin < 10 Gms/dL.\n 7. BUN [OR] or creatine above the normal limits.\n 8. Taking other drugs known to reduce the metabolism of minocycline and thus increase the probability of toxicity.<|eot_id|>']
Response: Inclusion Criteria:
 1. Taking a tetracycline within 6 months [OR] or history of adverse reaction to minocycline [OR] or another tetracycline.
 2. Enhanced risk from lumbar puncture, including documented [OR] or suspected cerebral mass lesion predisposing to brain herniation [OR] or bleeding diathesis.
 3. Pregnancy [OR] or expectation of pregnancy during the study.
 4. Active opportunistic infection [OR] or active neurological disease that might confound evaluation.
 5. ADC Stage > 1.
 6. Hemoglobin < 10 Gms/dL.
 7. BUN [OR] or creatine above the normal limits.
 8. Taking other drugs known to reduce the metabolism of minocycline and thus increase the probability of toxicity.
