] before words like "with", "to", "who", "in addition", "for", "and", "but", "that", "despite", "having".\n- Always write a [NOT] before "no", "not", "none",  "don\'t", "free", "prevent", "Inability", "lack", "impossible", "off", "without", "unable", "naive", "excluded", "absence"\n\nGeneral rules:\n- The operators [OR], [AND], [NOT] should be written before the words like or, and, not.\n- An [AND], [OR], [NOT] must not come before a line segment (\n).\n- Please carefully read through the given criteria text, then output only an edited version with the logical operators incorporated, enclosed in <EDITED_CRITERIA> tags.\n- Do not include any other explanations or output.\n\n\n### Input:\nInclusion Criteria:\n  -  1. Patient with hemodynamic corrected valvular atrial fibrillation who is scheduled for ablation procedure and ≥20 and ≤80 years of age\n  -  2. Left atrium size < 50mm\n  -  3. paroxysmal atrial fibrillation that is recurrence during antiarrhythmic drug treatment or is not able to use an antiarrhythmic drug.\n  -  4. Patient who is indicated for anticoagulation therapy (for prevention of cerebral infarction)\nExclusion Criteria:\n  -  1. Patients with permanent atrial fibrillation\n  -  2. Atrial fibrillation associated with severe cardiac malformation or a structural heart disease that is hemodynamically affected\n  -  3. Patients with severe renal impairment or CT imaging difficulty using contrast media\n  -  4. Patients with a past history of radiofrequency ablation for atrial fibrillation or other cardiac surgery\n  -  5. Patients with active internal bleeding\n  -  6. Patients with contraindications for anticoagulation therapy(for prevention of cerebral infarction) and antiarrhythmic drugs\n  -  7. Patients with non-valvular atrial fibrillation\n  -  8. Patients with a severe comorbid disease\n  -  9. Expected survival < 1 year\n  -  10. Drug addicts or alcoholics\n  -  11. Patients who cannot read the consent form (illiterates, foreigners, etc.)\n  -  12. Other patients who are judged by the principal or sub-investigator to be ineligible for participation in this clinical study\n\n### Response:\nInclusion Criteria:\n - 1. Patient with hemodynamic corrected valvular atrial fibrillation [AND] who is scheduled for ablation procedure [AND] and ≥20 [AND] and ≤80 years of age\n - 2. Left atrium size < 50mm\n - 3. paroxysmal atrial fibrillation that is recurrence during antiarrhythmic drug treatment [OR] or is [NOT] not able to use an antiarrhythmic drug.\n - 4. Patient who is indicated for anticoagulation therapy (for prevention of cerebral infarction)\nExclusion Criteria:\n - 1. Patients with permanent atrial fibrillation\n - 2. Atrial fibrillation associated with severe cardiac malformation [OR] or a structural heart disease that is hemodynamically affected\n - 3. Patients with severe renal impairment [OR] or CT imaging [NOT] difficulty using contrast media\n - 4. Patients with a past history of radiofrequency ablation for atrial fibrillation [OR] or other cardiac surgery\n - 5. Patients with active internal bleeding\n - 6. Patients with contraindications for anticoagulation therapy (for prevention of cerebral infarction) [OR] and antiarrhythmic drugs\n - 7. Patients with [NOT] non-valvular atrial fibrillation\n - 8. Patients with a severe comorbid disease\n - 9. Expected survival < 1 year\n - 10. Drug addicts [OR] or alcoholics\n - 11. Patients who cannot read the consent form (illiterates, foreigners, etc.)\n - 12. Other patients who are judged by the principal or sub-investigator to be ineligible for participation in this clinical study<|eot_id|>']
Response: Inclusion Criteria:
  -  Patients admitted to the hospital
Exclusion Criteria:
  -  None

### Instruction:
Create an edited version of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria that incorporates the logical operators [AND], [OR], [NOT].
Apply these rules for inserting the logical operators:
- Always write an [OR] before an "or", "and/or", "and / or" in the text.
- Always write an [OR] before a comma "," and before "/" in the text.
- Always write an [AND] before words like "with", "to", "who", "in addition", "for", "and", "but", "that", "despite", "having".
- Always write a [NOT] before "no", "not", "none",  "don't", "free", "prevent", "Inability", "lack", "impossible", "off", "without", "unable", "naive", "excluded", "absence"

General rules:
- The operators [OR], [AND], [NOT] should be written before the words like or, and, not.
- An [AND], [OR], [NOT] must not come before a line segment (
).
- Please carefully read through the given criteria text, then output only an edited version with the logical operators incorporated, enclosed in <EDITED_CRITERIA> tags.
- Do not include any other explanations or output.


### Input:
Inclusion Criteria:
  -  1. Patient with hemodynamic corrected valvular atrial fibrillation who is scheduled for ablation procedure and ≥20 and ≤80 years of age
  -  2. Left atrium size < 50mm
  -  3. paroxysmal atrial fibrillation that is recurrence during antiarrhythmic drug treatment or is not able to use an antiarrhythmic drug.
  -  4. Patient who is indicated for anticoagulation therapy (for prevention of cerebral infarction)
Exclusion Criteria:
  -  1. Patients with permanent atrial fibrillation
  -  2. Atrial fibrillation associated with severe cardiac malformation or a structural heart disease that is hemodynamically affected
  -  3. Patients with severe renal impairment or CT imaging difficulty using contrast media
  -  4. Patients with a past history of radiofrequency ablation for atrial fibrillation or other cardiac surgery
  -  5. Patients with active internal bleeding
  -  6. Patients with contraindications for anticoagulation therapy(for prevention of cerebral infarction) and antiarrhythmic drugs
  -  7. Patients with non-valvular atrial fibrillation
  -  8. Patients with a severe comorbid disease
  -  9. Expected survival < 1 year
  -  10. Drug addicts or alcoholics
  -  11. Patients who cannot read the consent form (illiterates, foreigners, etc.)
  -  12. Other patients who are judged by the principal or sub-investigator to be ineligible for participation in this clinical study
