Output: ['<|begin_of_text|>Below is an instruction that describes a task, paired with an input that provides further context. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.\n\n### Instruction:\nCreate an edited version of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria that incorporates the logical operators [AND], [OR], [NOT].\nApply these rules for inserting the logical operators:\n- Always write an [OR] before an "or", "and/or", "and / or" in the text.\n- Always write an [OR] before a comma "," and before "/" in the text.\n- Always write an [AND] before words like "with", "to", "who", "in addition", "for", "and", "but", "that", "despite", "having", "of", "on", "may", "as", "within", "by", "requiring", "should", "must have", "during", "have", "must", "are", "due", "according", "corresponding", "including".\n- Always write an [AND] after "history of".\n- Always write a [NOT] before "no", "not", "do not", "other than", "none",  "don\'t", "free", "besides", "prevent", "Inability", "absence", "impossible", "off", "without", "unable", "naive", "excluded", "absence"\n\nGeneral rules:\n- The operators [OR], [AND], [NOT] should be written before the words like or, and, not.\n- An [AND], [OR], [NOT] must not come before a line segment (\\n).\n- Please carefully read through the given criteria text, then output only an edited version with the logical operators incorporated, enclosed in <EDITED_CRITERIA> tags.\n- Do not include any other explanations or output.\n\n\n### Input:\nActive opioid dependence\nAcute or chronic pain requiring opioid treatment\nAcute liver injury (liver aminotransferase concentrations >5 times the upper limit of normal)\nHealth condition considered unsafe for inclusion (at discretion of PI and/or attending physician)\nLack of capacity or willingness to consent\nCurrently prescribed pharmacotherapy for alcohol dependence (not including treatment of acute alcohol withdrawal syndrome)\nPrevious significant adverse reaction to naltrexone or diluent\nPregnant, nursing, or not using effective methods of birth control\nPrisoners (as defined by Office of Human Research Protection) at the time of enrollment ARE NOT ELIGIBLE for study entry. However, subjects who become prisoners after being enrolled will be included and not be withdrawn from the study. Patients on parole or probation are eligible for enrollment.\n\n\n### Response:\nActive opioid dependence\nAcute [OR] or chronic pain requiring opioid treatment\nAcute liver injury (liver aminotransferase concentrations >5 times the upper limit of normal)\nHealth condition considered unsafe for inclusion (at discretion of PI and/or attending physician)\nLack of capacity or willingness to consent\nCurrently prescribed pharmacotherapy for alcohol dependence (not including treatment of acute alcohol withdrawal syndrome)\nPrevious significant adverse reaction to naltrexone [OR] or diluent\nPregnant, [OR] nursing, [OR] or [NOT] not using effective methods of birth control\nPrisoners (as defined by Office of Human Research Protection) at the time of enrollment ARE [NOT] NOT ELIGIBLE for study entry. However, subjects who become prisoners after being enrolled will be included and [NOT] not be withdrawn from the study. Patients on parole [OR] or probation are eligible for enrollment.<|eot_id|>']
Response: Active opioid dependence
Acute [OR] or chronic pain requiring opioid treatment
Acute liver injury (liver aminotransferase concentrations >5 times the upper limit of normal)
Health condition considered unsafe for inclusion (at discretion of PI and/or attending physician)
Lack of capacity or willingness to consent
Currently prescribed pharmacotherapy for alcohol dependence (not including treatment of acute alcohol withdrawal syndrome)
Previous significant adverse reaction to naltrexone [OR] or diluent
Pregnant, [OR] nursing, [OR] or [NOT] not using effective methods of birth control
Prisoners (as defined by Office of Human Research Protection) at the time of enrollment ARE [NOT] NOT ELIGIBLE for study entry. However, subjects who become prisoners after being enrolled will be included and [NOT] not be withdrawn from the study. Patients on parole [OR] or probation are eligible for enrollment.
